1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c5 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. dxc5 d4 7. Na4 Bxc5 8.
Nxc5 Qa5+ 9. Qd2 Qxc5 10. e3 dxe3 11. Qxe3+ Qxe3+ 12. Bxe3 Bg4 13. Bb5 Bxf3 14.
gxf3 Nge7 15. Rd1 a6 16. Ba4 b5 {"The game I won against May in 1963, was
pretty dubious. From a simple slightly worse position, I won in another 17
moves! I remember putting pawns on f5 and g6, blocking one of his bishops, and
safe from the other. I cannot reproduce the missing moves. I remember at the
lunch break (!) telling my father that I could see how to win a pawn, but that
would only draw, so I planned instead to keep the position closed." (Chris
Wood, email to JS, 27 Oct 2005)} 17. Bc2 O-O 18. O-O Rfd8 19. Be4 f5 20. Bb1
Ne5 21. Kg2 Nc4 22. Bc1 g6 23. a4 Kf7 24. Rfe1 Nc6 25. Ba2 Rxd1 26. Rxd1 Rd8
27. Rxd8 Nxd8 28. Bd2 Ke6 29. axb5 axb5 30. Bc3 Nc6 31. h4 Kd5 32. Kg3 b4 33.
Bg7 Nd4 34. Kf4 Ne6+ {Source: CHESS, March 1963, p189} 0-1

